/K [5]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (The problems with the current digital library systems include the fact that book distribution and management are centralized, slow, and vulnerable to piracy. There is an increasing number of copyright violations, unfair royalty distribution, and data breaches in existing systems. The involvement of intermediaries such as publishers or hosting platforms often leads to reduced author profits and lack of transparency. One major drawback of the current digital library system is that verifying ownership or tracking digital book access requires extensive manual work, making the process inefficient. Present systems also fail to ensure fair compensation for authors and do not prevent duplication or unauthorized sharing of books. These systems rely on centralized databases, which are prone to manipulation and data loss. Currently, most libraries and e-book platforms store data through centralized servers, which makes them susceptible to hacking and piracy. Moreover, the lack of a transparent verification method makes it difficult to authenticate book ownership, ensure fair payments, and maintain trust between authors, publishers, and readers.)
/E (The problems with the current digital library systems include the fact that book distribution and management are centralized, slow, and vulnerable to piracy. There is an increasing number of copyright violations, unfair royalty distribution, and data breaches in existing systems. The involvement of intermediaries such as publishers or hosting platforms often leads to reduced author profits and lack of transparency. One major drawback of the current digital library system is that verifying ownership or tracking digital book access requires extensive manual work, making the process inefficient. Present systems also fail to ensure fair compensation for authors and do not prevent duplication or unauthorized sharing of books. These systems rely on centralized databases, which are prone to manipulation and data loss. Currently, most libraries and e-book platforms store data through centralized servers, which makes them susceptible to hacking and piracy. Moreover, the lack of a transparent verification method makes it difficult to authenticate book ownership, ensure fair payments, and maintain trust between authors, publishers, and readers.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
